[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Flutter: Hero Animations, Physics, and Gesture Handling"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Creating fluid, delightful user experiences in Flutter requires mastering three pillars of modern UI development: Hero animations for seamless screen transitions, custom physics for natural-feeling scrolling and snapping, and advanced gesture handling for interactive manipulation."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Implementing Hero Animations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hero animations provide a visual bridge between two different screens. When a user taps an item, a shared widget flies across the screen, morphing into its new layout."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Source Screen
Hero(
  tag: 'product-image',
  child: ClipRRect(
    borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(8.0),
    child: Image.asset('assets/product.png', width: 100, height: 100),
  ),
)

// Destination Screen
Scaffold(
  body: Center(
    child: Hero(
      tag: 'product-image',
      child: Image.asset('assets/product.png', width: 300, height: 300),
    ),
  ),
)"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Customizing Physics in Flutter"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Flutter's ScrollPhysics dictate how lists and scrollables respond to user input. By extending 'ScrollPhysics', you can create custom spring behaviors, edge resistance, and snapping effects."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class CustomSpringPhysics extends ScrollPhysics {
  const CustomSpringPhysics({ScrollPhysics? parent}) : super(parent: parent);

  @override
  CustomSpringPhysics applyTo(ScrollPhysics? ancestor) {
    return CustomSpringPhysics(parent: buildParent(ancestor));
  }

  @override
  SpringDescription get spring => const SpringDescription(
        mass: 50,
        stiffness: 100,
        damping: 0.8,
      );
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Advanced Gesture Handling with GestureDetector and RawGestureDetector"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To handle complex multi-touch interactions, panning, zooming, and rotations simultaneously, you need to look beyond the standard 'GestureDetector' and understand Flutter's gesture arena using 'RawGestureDetector'."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"GestureDetector(
  onScaleUpdate: (ScaleUpdateDetails details) {
    setState(() {
      _scale = _previousScale * details.scale;
      _position += details.focalPointDelta;
    });
  },
  onScaleStart: (ScaleStartDetails details) {
    _previousScale = _scale;
  },
  child: Transform(
    transform: Matrix4.identity()
      ..translate(_position.dx, _position.dy)
      ..scale(_scale),
    child: const FlutterLogo(size: 200),
  ),
)"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices for Performance"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always wrap costly animations inside RepaintBoundary to avoid rebuilding the entire widget tree."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use FlightShuttleBuilder in Hero widgets to customize the transition widget separately from the source and destination."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Profile your physics animations using the Flutter DevTools Performance overlay to maintain a steady 60 or 120 FPS."}]
